# ant serial capture v1: <seconds|-> <dir> <framed message, hex>
# HAND-ASSEMBLED, NOT A CAPTURE. See tools/vectors/README.md.
#
# This exists so the harness exercises the real-timestamp form of the column as
# well as the '-' form; tools/ant_trace.py writes real timestamps and
# tools/ant_conformance.py writes '-'.
#
# It is archive/captures/serial/README.md's own worked example, corrected. That
# transcript prints three lines and two of them do not check out:
#
#   0.014200 < a4016f20fa    A4 ^ 01 ^ 6F ^ 20 = EA, not FA
#   0.014850 > a4024d005400bf   LEN = 2 puts the checksum at index 5, which is
#                               0x00, where the XOR through index 4 is 0xBF
#
# Both are typos in an illustrative example rather than anything that was ever
# on a wire, but they are the reason this directory exists: bytes typed by hand
# into prose drift, and only something that replays them notices. The bad forms
# are kept as rejection cases in tools/test_ant_wire.py.
#
# The timings are plausible spacing, not measurements. Nothing may be inferred
# from them.

# case reset
#   A4 ^ 01 = A5   A5 ^ 4A = EF   EF ^ 00 = EF
0.000000 > a4014a00ef
#   A4 ^ 01 = A5   A5 ^ 6F = CA   CA ^ 20 = EA
0.014200 < a4016f20ea

# case capabilities
#   A4 ^ 02 = A6   A6 ^ 4D = EB   EB ^ 00 = EB   EB ^ 54 = BF
0.014850 > a4024d0054bf
#   A4^09=AD  ^54=F9  ^08=F1  ^08=F9  ^00=F9  ^B2=4B
#   ^32=79    ^00=79  ^FD=84  ^8D=09  ^0F=06
0.016100 < a40954080800b23200fd8d0f06
